BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 248.0 Td (  In the dawn of the First Age, two spirits, Aethel and Thorne, guarded the valley of the Silver) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 238.0 Td (     Mist. Aethel held five golden embers, the seeds of prosperity. Thorne held the fertile, emp) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 228.0 Td (    ty soil of the earth. Knowing that a solitary ember would dim, Aethel cast her five sparks i) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 218.0 Td (    nto the dark loam. Upon touching the soil, the embers ignited, expanding and weaving into a ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 208.0 Td (    shimmering canopy of light, tripling in brilliance. Thorne watched the glow, his heart stirr) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 198.0 Td (    ed by the gift. He knew the embers could grant him power, but he also remembered the bridge ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 188.0 Td (    Aethel had built. He gathered the light, keeping only what was necessary to sustain the vall) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 178.0 Td (    ey, and returned the surplus to Aethel. As the embers flowed back, they turned into golden f) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 168.0 Td (    ruit, nourishing both spirits. They learned that the earth yielded nothing for the greedy, b) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 158.0 Td (    ut bloomed in abundance for those who understood the cycle of the gift. From that day, the v) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 148.0 Td (    alley flourished, for Aethel and Thorne realized that to hold onto the light was to lose it,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 138.0 Td (     but to cast it forward was to possess the world. Their trust became the foundation of all l) Tj ET
