BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 668.0 Td (      xistence-the Great Reciprocity. Trust became the gravity that held the stars in place. To ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 658.0 Td (      give is to sow, and to return is to harvest, creating a cycle that forever banished the sh) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 648.0 Td (      adows of the lonely Void.) Tj ET
BT /F2 8.0 Tf 48.0 638.0 Td (  Agent_6 \(Finn\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 628.0 Td (    In the primordial era, the Great Orchard was barren, for the spirits feared scarcity. A trav) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 618.0 Td (      eler, carrying a pouch of five golden acorns, met the Gatekeeper at the edge of the grove.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (       The traveler understood the Law of the Orchard: to plant was to invite the unseen spirits) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (       to triple the harvest.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 578.0 Td (    With a steady heart, the traveler surrendered all five acorns to the Gatekeeper. The spirits) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 568.0 Td (       watched, for this was the ultimate offering. In their hands, the five acorns shimmered, e) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 558.0 Td (      xpanding into fifteen through the grace of the soil. The Gatekeeper, moved by the traveler) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 548.0 Td (      's radical trust, returned ten golden acorns, keeping five for the labor of watering.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 538.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 528.0 Td (    Thus, both flourished. The traveler departed with double their initial wealth, and the Gatek) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 518.0 Td (      eeper cultivated a garden of abundance. But once, a greedy soul withheld the seeds, and th) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 508.0 Td (      e spirits withdrew their favor. The garden withered, leaving only dust. The lesson endures) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 498.0 Td (      : when one offers their all, they invite the universe to multiply their fortune. Trust is ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 488.0 Td (      not a gamble, but the foundation of the infinite harvest. Only through the exchange of the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 478.0 Td (       soul's bounty does the orchard grow, and only through reciprocity does the cycle remain w) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (      hole and ever-giving.) Tj ET
BT /F2 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (  Agent_7 \(Galen\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (    In the dawn of the First Age, two spirits, Aethel and Thorne, guarded the valley of the Silv) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (      er Mist. Aethel held five golden embers, the seeds of prosperity. Thorne held the fertile,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (       empty soil of the earth. Knowing that a solitary ember would dim, Aethel cast her five sp) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 418.0 Td (      arks into the dark loam. Upon touching the soil, the embers ignited, expanding and weaving) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 408.0 Td (       into a shimmering canopy of light, tripling in brilliance. Thorne watched the glow, his h) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 398.0 Td (      eart stirred by the gift. He knew the embers could grant him power, but he also remembered) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 388.0 Td (       the bridge Aethel had built. He gathered the light, keeping only what was necessary to su) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (      stain the valley, and returned the surplus to Aethel. As the embers flowed back, they turn) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (      ed into golden fruit, nourishing both spirits. They learned that the earth yielded nothing) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 358.0 Td (       for the greedy, but bloomed in abundance for those who understood the cycle of the gift. ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 348.0 Td (      From that day, the valley flourished, for Aethel and Thorne realized that to hold onto the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 338.0 Td (       light was to lose it, but to cast it forward was to possess the world. Their trust became) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 328.0 Td (       the foundation of all life, a dance of return that kept the stars burning bright.) Tj ET
BT /F2 8.0 Tf 48.0 318.0 Td (  Agent_8 \(Hana\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 308.0 Td (    In the beginning, the Weaver and the Merchant lived in separate valleys. The Weaver had silk) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 298.0 Td (      ; the Merchant had grain. They stood at the edge of the chasm, holding their offerings. Th) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 288.0 Td (      e Weaver feared the Merchant would take the silk without returning grain. The Merchant fea) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 278.0 Td (      red the Weaver would keep the grain and offer nothing. But one day, the Weaver tossed her ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 268.0 Td (      silk across the void. It doubled in the air, catching the light of the sun-a gift of faith) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 258.0 Td (      . The Merchant, seeing this, did not hoard. He returned a portion of the doubled grain bac) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 248.0 Td (      k across the chasm. Through this act of vulnerability, the void between them vanished, and) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 238.0 Td (       the fields flourished. ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 228.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 218.0 Td (    They learned that the secret of growth lay not in keeping what one has, but in the gamble of) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 208.0 Td (       connection. The wind carries their echo: "He who holds his hand closed finds only dust; h) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 198.0 Td (      e who opens it to a stranger receives the harvest of the world." Trust is the bridge, and ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 188.0 Td (      reciprocity is the stone upon which it stands. They realized that when one gives with an o) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 178.0 Td (      pen heart, the world responds with abundance, proving that mutual prosperity requires the ) Tj ET
